---
title: Oxlint Plugin for Nx
description: Set up and use the @nx/oxlint plugin with inferred tasks, hybrid ESLint migration, and experimental module-boundary enforcement.
sidebar:
  label: Introduction
---

The `@nx/oxlint` plugin runs [Oxlint](https://oxc.rs/docs/guide/usage/linter/) as a cached Nx task.

{% aside type="caution" title="Experimental" %}
`@nx/oxlint` is experimental. The module boundaries bridge depends on Oxlint's JavaScript plugin API, which Oxlint excludes from its semantic versioning policy and can change in any release.
{% /aside %}

## Set up

```shell
nx add @nx/oxlint
```

This installs `oxlint`, registers `@nx/oxlint/plugin`, and creates a root `.oxlintrc.json` if the workspace has no Oxlint config.

## Inferred tasks

`@nx/oxlint/plugin` infers a lint task for projects governed by an Oxlint config. It recognizes the four config files Oxlint discovers on its own:

- `.oxlintrc.json`
- `.oxlintrc.jsonc`
- `oxlint.config.ts`
- `oxlint.config.mts`

A project only gets a task when it contains files Oxlint can lint, so documentation-only and non-JavaScript projects stay untouched.

View the inferred task with:

```shell
nx show project my-project --web
```

### Task inputs

Inferred tasks declare the Oxlint config, every config reachable through `extends`, and any `tsconfig.json` referenced from outside the project root. Changing any of them invalidates the cache.

## Editor setup

Install the official [Oxc extension](https://marketplace.visualstudio.com/items?itemName=oxc.oxc-vscode) (`oxc.oxc-vscode`) for VS Code and Cursor. `nx add @nx/oxlint` appends it to `.vscode/extensions.json` when that file already exists.

Oxc also ships extensions for Zed, JetBrains IDEs and Neovim. Any editor with LSP support can use `oxlint --lsp` as its language server.

## Config file format

Oxlint reads the first config it finds, and only one per directory. Prefer `.oxlintrc.json`:

- `oxlint.config.ts` needs a Node runtime that executes TypeScript (Node 22.18 or later), and doesn't work with the standalone binary.
- Only JSON configs get their `extends` chain tracked as task inputs. Nx can't read a TypeScript config statically.

## Task naming

Oxlint is designed to run alongside ESLint during a migration, so the plugin claims the first available name from:

1. `lint`
2. `oxlint`
3. `oxlint:lint`
4. `oxlint-lint`

A workspace with no other linter gets `lint`. A workspace where ESLint already owns `lint` gets `oxlint`.

## Explicit tasks

When `useInferencePlugins` is disabled, use the executor directly:

```json
{
  "targets": {
    "lint": {
      "executor": "@nx/oxlint:lint",
      "options": {
        "lintFilePatterns": ["{projectRoot}"]
      }
    }
  }
}
```

## Add Oxlint to an ESLint workspace

```shell
nx g @nx/oxlint:convert-from-eslint
```

This adds an `oxlint` task to every project that has an ESLint task and leaves the ESLint configuration in place. It doesn't translate ESLint rules into Oxlint rules. To generate an `.oxlintrc.json` from an existing ESLint config, run [`@oxlint/migrate`](https://github.com/oxc-project/oxlint-migrate) separately. To stop ESLint from re-reporting what Oxlint already covers, add [`eslint-plugin-oxlint`](https://www.npmjs.com/package/eslint-plugin-oxlint) to the ESLint config.

## Type-aware linting

Oxlint's type-aware rules need the `oxlint-tsgolint` package and TypeScript 7. Enable them per task with the `typeAware` option, and point `tsconfig` at the project's TypeScript config.

## Module boundaries

`@nx/oxlint` exposes the project-graph-aware `enforce-module-boundaries` rule as an Oxlint JavaScript plugin. Install `@nx/eslint-plugin`, then register the bridge in `.oxlintrc.json`:

```json
{
  "jsPlugins": ["@nx/oxlint/boundaries-plugin"],
  "rules": {
    "@nx/enforce-module-boundaries": [
      "error",
      {
        "depConstraints": []
      }
    ]
  }
}
```

The rule runs as JavaScript rather than native Rust, so it's slower than Oxlint's built-in rules.

{% aside type="note" title="Dependency checks aren't available" %}
Oxlint doesn't lint JSON files, so the `@nx/dependency-checks` rule cannot run on Oxlint. Keep ESLint for that check.
{% /aside %}
